    "description": "Business-to-business marketplaces connect commercial buyers with suppliers rather than focusing solely on household purchases. Inspected Iranian examples span wholesale goods and industrial equipment, but their public descriptions do not by themselves establish transaction volumes or marketplace guarantees.",
    "tags": ["B2B", "Wholesale", "Industrial equipment", "Procurement", "Marketplaces"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T18:59:20Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T18:59:20Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "B2B Marketplaces in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iranian business sourcing through wholesale catalogues and industrial-equipment platforms, with clear limits on transaction and performance claims."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "commercial-scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A marketplace for business purchasing",
        "content": "Business-to-business, abbreviated B2B, describes commerce between businesses. In the Iranian examples inspected here, the term encompasses substantially different product fields. Buskool presents itself as a wholesale buying-and-selling market. Vitrinnet's company profile describes a platform for industrial equipment and machinery. These examples support a distinction between wholesale merchandise sourcing and equipment procurement; they do not establish that either platform represents the entire national market. [s1][s2]\n\nThe difference matters when interpreting a marketplace page. A catalogue can reveal what kinds of commercial needs a platform addresses, while leaving the transaction itself largely unexplained. Product visibility, supplier introduction, payment handling, and responsibility for delivery are separate questions. Neither a wholesale label nor an industrial-equipment description should be read as proof that a platform performs all of these functions. The inspected sources establish positioning and advertised scope, with important contractual questions still unresolved. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "wholesale-range",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Wholesale goods across different supply chains",
        "content": "Buskool's visible category directory includes agricultural goods such as rice, dates, pistachios, fruit, and pulses. It also includes processed foods, livestock-related products, metals, chemical materials, and recyclable scrap. This breadth shows that a wholesale marketplace can organize goods associated with very different supply chains rather than functioning as a single-industry shop. [s1]\n\nThe directory is useful evidence of how the platform classifies its offering. It is weaker evidence of actual trade: a category's presence does not establish completed orders, available quantities, supplier reliability, or national coverage. Agricultural and industrial headings also cannot be converted into market-share estimates. This page therefore treats Buskool as an observed example of wholesale catalogue organization, without assigning it a competitive rank or attributing unverified transaction safeguards to it. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "equipment-and-information",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Industrial equipment and the information layer",
        "content": "Vitrinnet's public company profile identifies industrial equipment and machinery as its focus. It says the platform provides current prices, specialist consultation, and industry content to simplify buying and selling for suppliers and buyers. This is the company's account of its intended value: information and consultation accompany the product offering. The profile does not independently demonstrate that quoted prices remain available or that consultation improves procurement outcomes. [s2]\n\nAn analytical distinction follows from this positioning. A platform may help a business discover equipment and understand an offering before any purchase is completed. That information role should be evaluated separately from financing, installation, maintenance, or dispute resolution. Those additional responsibilities were not established by the inspected profile. Vitrinnet's main website could not be retrieved during this research, limiting verification of its detailed terms and current operating model. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "infrastructure-and-limits",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Digital sourcing still depends on access",
        "content": "A digital catalogue does not remove the infrastructure conditions surrounding its use. A research commentary published by Internet Society Pulse describes how Iranian filtering policies, international sanctions affecting online services, and network-performance problems can reduce internet reliability. It also notes that users may struggle to distinguish filtering from geoblocking or technical failure. These findings concern the broader internet environment; they are not measurements of Buskool or Vitrinnet uptime. [s3]\n\nThe supported picture is therefore one of differentiated sourcing models with incomplete public performance evidence. Wholesale category breadth and equipment-focused consultation explain different aspects of B2B commerce. Establishing how effectively either model converts discovery into dependable supply requires transaction evidence and contractual documentation beyond the material inspected here. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
